+lesson:
+  introduction: |-
+    How can Coulomb's law for a point charge predict the field of a rod or a disk? Replace the object with small charged pieces, add their vector fields, and take the limit as the pieces become infinitesimal. The integral is the final expression of that construction.
+
+    You will build charge elements, resolve their fields, and evaluate rod, ring, and disk integrals. You need definite integrals, simple substitution, and vector components. We assume fixed charges in vacuum, use $k=1/(4\pi\varepsilon_0)$, and evaluate fields away from the idealized charged material. Charge densities are signed; the diagrams illustrate positive charge.
+  sections:
+  - id: physics2-ch05-coulombs-law-s1
+    title: Point-charge interaction
+    required: true
+    content: Start with two signed point charges. The force on $q_2$ due to $q_1$ has magnitude $F=k|q_1q_2|/r^2$
+      and points along the line joining them. A positive product means repulsion; a negative product means attraction.
+      The scalar magnitude is never a substitute for deciding direction.
+    media: []
+    check:
+      id: physics2-ch05-coulombs-law-s1-check
+      type: multipleChoice
+      prompt: 'Two charges $q_1=+2\,\mu\mathrm C$ and $q_2=-3\,\mu\mathrm C$ are separated by $r$. Which direction
+        is the force on $q_2$?'
+      choices:
+      - id: a
+        text: away from $q_1$
+        media: []
+        issueSignals:
+        - id: sign-error
+          domains: []
+      - id: b
+        text: toward $q_1$
+        media: []
+        issueSignals: []
+      - id: c
+        text: perpendicular to the line
+        media: []
